.TH OTPME-DEPLOY 1 "2026-09-18" "OTPme 0.3.0a278" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-deploy \- manage configuration jobs for hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-deploy
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-deploy
creates configuration jobs for hosts and shows their results.
A job does one thing on one or more hosts, for example install a package, add an apt repository or restart a service.
.PP
Every job is signed with the sign key of the user who creates it.
Before you sign, OTPme shows you what the job does.
The host checks the signature before it runs the job.
.PP
Two checks must pass before a job runs:
.IP 1. 4
The nodes check that you may create jobs for the host.
This is set with the config parameter \fBallow_confd_jobs\fR.
.IP 2. 4
The host checks that the job is signed by a signer it trusts for this job type.
Signers are added on the host with \fBotpme\-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type\fR \fIjob_type\fR.
.PP
A host without a signer for the job type refuses the job and says so.
A refusal is not a run: the job is still there, and it runs as soon as the reason is gone.
Add the missing signer and the host does the job, you do not need a new one.
See \fBRefused jobs\fR.
.PP
You name the hosts of a job with
.BR \-\-host ,
.BR \-\-unit ,
.B \-\-role
or
.BR \-\-group .
To find out which hosts need the job, ask
.BR otpme\-inventory (1):
it knows which host has which package version, and what each machine is made of.
See \fBFinding the hosts\fR under \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.SS Who can see and change jobs
Admins can see and change all jobs.
Other users see a job if they may create jobs for at least one of its hosts.
They only see the state and the results of those hosts.
The user who created a job sees all of its hosts.
.PP
\fBack\fR and \fBcancel\fR change the whole job.
They are allowed for the user who created the job, and for users who may create jobs for all of its hosts.
.SS How jobs run
On every host, the \fBconfd\fR daemon asks the nodes of its site for new jobs every 60 seconds.
It runs one job at a time.
A \fBremote_access\fR job is the exception.
It is a shell for a user, not work on the host.
So it runs beside other jobs, and other jobs run beside it.
A host runs up to 8 sessions at the same time.
.PP
Each job runs as its own transient systemd service (\fBotpme\-confd\-job\-\fIuuid\fB.service\fR).
This way a job keeps running when OTPme is restarted, even if the job restarts OTPme itself.
.PP
A job runs only once on each host.
If the result can not be sent, confd sends it again later.
It does not run the job again.
.PP
The full output of a job stays on the host in \fI/var/log/otpme/confd/\fIuuid\fI.log\fR.
The nodes get the last 64 KiB of the output.
.SS Refused jobs
A host refuses a job it may not or cannot run.
It may not run a job it holds no signer for, one whose signature does not hold, or one that would take away its last \fBsigner_mgmt\fR signer.
It cannot run a job that names an object it has not synced yet.
.PP
A refused job is not done.
Nothing ran, and the job can still run later, so the host does not put it away.
It reports the refusal once, so you see why nothing happened, and it keeps the job in view.
.PP
When the host may not run a job, it waits for its signers to change.
Add the signer it was missing, and the job runs with the next round.
.PP
When the host cannot run a job, it asks for the job again every round.
Nothing tells a host that a user or a role has arrived, so it tries until it can.
This needs nothing from you: the sync brings the object, and the next round runs the job.
.PP
A failed job is done.
It ran, and it did not work.
It is never run again, on that host you need a new job.
.PP
\fBcancel\fR ends the waiting.
A job that was taken back is not offered to a host anymore, so a host that refused it never sees it again.
The refusal it reported stays, and \fBshow\fR names it next to the cancellation: the host saw the job and said no, and then the job was withdrawn.
.PP
\fBshow\fR and \fBresults\fR name both states, and \fBack\fR takes both.
.SS How long jobs are kept
Jobs and their results stay on the nodes for 90 days (site parameter \fBconf_job_max_age\fR).
This also applies to jobs that worked, so you can see which jobs were already sent.
.PP
A job with something unanswered about it stays until you acknowledge it with \fBack\fR, however old it is.
After that it is removed when it is older than \fBconf_job_max_age\fR.
.PP
Unanswered is a host where the job failed, a host that refused it, and a host that never reported at all.
The last one is what nothing else would tell you about: a host that was switched off for three months is still waiting for that job, and the job waits with it.
.PP
Two kinds of job are not kept that way.
.PP
One you took back with \fBcancel\fR: you looked at it when you did that, and the hosts that never ran it were never meant to.
That answers the whole job, a host where it failed before you took it back included \(em what that host did is in its result either way, and a job that shows as over must not be the one line that is still waiting for somebody.
.PP
And a \fBremote_access\fR job, which is a shell somebody was waiting for.
.B otpme\-shell
stands there until the host comes and says what came of it, so there is nothing to find out later \(em and a job is made for every shell, so held like the others they would pile up.
.PP
Such a job is not kept for 90 days either.
It is removed an hour after it expired, which is the site parameter \fBremote_access_job_max_age\fR; counted from the expiry, because that is when the shell stopped being anybody's business.
Set it to \fB0\fR to have them go as soon as they expire.
.PP
For every other job type an expired job is not an exception.
Every job has an \fB\-\-expiry\fR and the default is 30 days, which is less than the 90 a job is kept \(em so by the time anything looks at an old job it has expired, and that says nothing about whether anybody saw it.
.PP
.B otpme\-deploy list \-\-pending
shows what is still waiting for a host.
.PP
If a host is deleted before it ran a job, the job does not wait for it.
The host is shown as deleted.
.PP
On the host, confd keeps the job logs for 90 days.
.SH JOB TYPES
Each job has a job type and an action.
The job type is also the signer type the host checks against.
Arguments are given with \fB\-\-arg\fR \fIname\fB=\fIvalue\fR.
A value with commas is a list.
.TP
.B sw_mgmt
Manage software packages.
.RS
.TP
.B install packages=\fIpkg1,pkg2\fR
Install packages.
.TP
.B remove packages=\fIpkg1,pkg2\fR [\fBautoremove=yes\fR]
Remove packages. With \fBautoremove=yes\fR, packages that are no longer needed are removed too.
.TP
.B upgrade packages=\fIpkg1,pkg2\fR
Upgrade installed packages.
.TP
.B dist_upgrade
Update the package lists, then upgrade every package.
Stops if the update fails.
.TP
.B autoremove
Remove packages that are no longer needed.
.RE
.TP
.B repo_mgmt
Manage apt repositories.
.RS
.TP
.B add_repo name=\fIname\fB line=\fIline\fR \fB\-\-key\fR \fIkey_file\fR
Add a repository and its signing key, then update the package lists.
\fIline\fR is a normal sources.list line without options, for example \fB"deb https://repo.example.org/debian bookworm main"\fR.
The key is required.
.TP
.B del_repo name=\fIname\fR
Remove a repository that was added with \fBadd_repo\fR, then update the package lists.
.TP
.B update
Update the package lists.
.RE
.TP
.B signer_mgmt
Manage the signers of the host.
.RS
.TP
.B show_signer \fR[\fBtype=\fIsigner_type\fR]
Show the signers the host holds, or only those of one type.
The answer is the output of the job, so
.B otpme\-deploy results
shows it.
This action changes nothing.
.TP
.B add_signer type=\fIsigner_type\fB signer=\fIobject_id\fR [\fBtags=\fItag1,tag2\fR] [\fBverify_token_opts=\fIyes\fR] [\fBallow_throwaway_key=\fIyes\fR]
Add a system-wide signer. \fIobject_id\fR is the OID of a user or role, for example \fBrole|example.org/berlin/sw\-admins\fR.
The signer is pinned to the current key of the user or role.
If the signer exists already, nothing changes.
.RS
.PP
\fBverify_token_opts\fR (default yes) requires the signature of an SSH token to cover the key options the key is used with, for example \fBcommand=\fR.
.PP
\fBallow_throwaway_key\fR (default no) is only for a \fBremote_access\fR signer.
It lets that signer ask for a shell on a key the job brings along, instead of on a key the host already holds.
See
.BR otpme\-shell (1).
.PP
The host needs the user or the role itself for this, not only its name: it pins a key, and the key is in the object.
A host that has not synced the object yet refuses the job and runs it as soon as the object is there.
This happens with a user or a role that was just created, and it sorts itself out.
.PP
A name nobody ever had is a different thing, and \fBadd\fR says so right away instead of letting every host refuse the job for ever.
.RE
.TP
.B del_signer type=\fIsigner_type\fB signer=\fIobject_id\fR [\fBremove_last_own_signer=\fIyes\fR]
Remove a signer. If there is no such signer, nothing changes.
.br
The user or role does not have to exist any more \(em see below.
.TP
.B update_signer type=\fIsigner_type\fB signer=\fIobject_id\fR [\fBremove_gone=\fIyes\fR] [\fBremove_last_own_signer=\fIyes\fR] [\fBverify_token_opts=\fIyes\fR] [\fBallow_throwaway_key=\fIyes\fR]
Pin the current key of the user or role again, for example after a key change.
.RS
.PP
\fBverify_token_opts\fR and \fBallow_throwaway_key\fR change what the signer may ask for.
A job that does not name them leaves them as they are: an update is usually about the pinned key, and saying nothing must not take a permission away.
This is the only way to change one of them without removing the signer and adding it again.
.PP
\fBdel_signer\fR and \fBupdate_signer\fR also take the UUID of the signer entry, as \fBshow_signer\fR prints it in its first column.
They name an entry on the host, and an entry outlives its user or role.
\fBadd_signer\fR does not take a UUID: it needs the object.
.PP
\fBdel_signer\fR takes the OID of a user or role that was deleted as well.
Each entry carries the OID it was added under, so the host finds it by that name even when nothing answers to it any more.
An object that was only renamed is found by its UUID as before, so the new name works and the old one does too.
.PP
\fBupdate_signer\fR is the one that needs the object: it pins the key the object has now.
For a signer whose user or role is gone, name the entry by its UUID.
.PP
A signer whose user or role was deleted is kept, and so is the key a role pinned for a member it does not have anymore.
What they signed is out there and still has to validate.
\fBremove_gone=yes\fR drops them, which is how their keys go once nothing needs them.
.RE
.PP
A host verifies a \fBsigner_mgmt\fR job against its \fBsigner_mgmt\fR signers, so such a job can take away the permission it runs under.
The host refuses a job that would leave no \fBsigner_mgmt\fR signer covering the user who signed it.
They could not send another job to that host, and putting a signer back is itself a \fBsigner_mgmt\fR job: somebody would have to walk to the host.
.PP
\fBremove_last_own_signer=yes\fR allows it.
Say it only when you mean to hand the host over, or when you are at its console.
The word is part of the job, so it stands in the tags you are shown before you sign.
.PP
These actions do the same as \fBotpme\-tool show_signer\fR, \fBadd_signer\fR, \fBdel_signer\fR and \fBupdate_signer\fR run as root on the host.
\fBotpme\-tool del_signer\fR on the host takes the UUID of the entry as well, which is the way out when the host cannot be reached by job anymore.
.RE
.TP
.B service_mgmt
Manage systemd services.
.RS
.TP
.B start\fR|\fBstop\fR|\fBrestart\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBdisable\fR \fBunit=\fIunit\fR
Run the action on a systemd unit.
.RE
.TP
.B file_mgmt
Manage files.
.RS
.TP
.B put_file path=\fIpath\fR \fB\-\-file\fR \fIsource_file\fR [\fBmode=\fImode\fR] [\fBowner=\fIuser\fR] [\fBgroup=\fIgroup\fR]
Write a file on the host.
The content comes from \fIsource_file\fR.
.TP
.B del_file path=\fIpath\fR
Remove a file.
.RE
.TP
.B host_mgmt
Switch the host.
.RS
.TP
.B reboot
Reboot the host after one minute.
.TP
.B shutdown
Switch the host off after one minute.
It stays off until somebody is standing next to it.
.PP
The delay lets the host send the result first.
Once the host is down, nobody would send it.
.PP
Upgrading the host is not here.
That is the package manager's work, see \fBsw_mgmt dist_upgrade\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B remote_access
Hand out a shell on the host.
.RS
.TP
.B shell \fR[\fBuser=\fIuser\fR] [\fBcommand=\fIcommand\fR] [\fBpubkey=\fIkey\fR]
The host connects out to the master node of its site and speaks SSH through it.
Without \fBpubkey\fR the host allows the keys of its own \fBauthorized_keys\fR for \fIuser\fR.
With \fBpubkey\fR it allows that one key and no other, and then the signature of the job is the only thing that gets somebody in.
The host takes such a job only from a signer added with \fBallow_throwaway_key\fR.
.PP
Do not create this job by hand.
Use
.BR otpme\-shell (1),
which creates it and then takes the shell.
.RE
.PP
Most of these job types give full control over the host, not only the ones that look like it.
.PP
\fBfile_mgmt\fR writes any file as root.
\fBsigner_mgmt\fR decides who may command the host at all.
\fBremote_access\fR hands out a root shell.
.PP
\fBsw_mgmt\fR and \fBrepo_mgmt\fR belong in the same group, which is less obvious: the maintainer scripts of a package run as root, and a repository says where packages come from.
Being allowed to install a package is being allowed to run code as root.
.PP
\fBservice_mgmt\fR starts and stops what is already on the host, and \fBhost_mgmt\fR only switches it off.
Those two cannot take the host over, but they can stop it doing its work.
.PP
So add signers only for users you trust with root access on that host.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add \fR[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost1,host2\fR] [\fB\-\-unit\fR \fIunit\fR] [\fB\-\-recursive\fR] [\fB\-\-role\fR \fIrole1,role2\fR] [\fB\-\-group\fR \fIgroup1,group2\fR] [\fB\-\-arg\fR \fIname=value\fR]... [\fB\-\-file\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-expiry\fR \fItime\fR] \fIjob_type\fR \fIaction\fR
Create a job and sign it.
You need at least one of \fB\-\-host\fR, \fB\-\-unit\fR, \fB\-\-role\fR or \fB\-\-group\fR. They can be combined, and a host named twice is taken once.
The command fails if a required argument is missing, if an argument is unknown, or if you may not create jobs for one of the hosts.
A \fBsigner_mgmt\fR job also fails here when it names a signer that is not a user or a role, or one that does not exist at all.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost\fR] [\fB\-\-failed\fR] [\fB\-\-pending\fR]
List jobs. Shows the time, the job type and action, the arguments, the number of hosts, the signer and the state of the job.
The colour says how the job stands:
grey when the job was taken back,
and otherwise the worst state first \(em
red when a host ran it and it did not work,
yellow when a host would not run it,
cyan when a host failed or refused and that was acknowledged with \fBack\fR,
orange while a host has not run it yet,
green when every host ran it.
.RS
.PP
Grey comes first because a job you took back is over and there is nothing to do about it.
Red would read as something going wrong, which is the opposite of what happened.
A host that ran the job before you took it back and failed is still in the state of the line (\fBcancelled, 2 ok, 1 failed\fR) and in \fBresults\fR.
Nothing waits for an \fBack\fR after that, see \fBHow long jobs are kept\fR.
.PP
\fBack\fR takes the place of red and yellow, so what somebody has already seen does not hide the line that is new.
It changes nothing about a job that worked or one that is still running.
.RE
.TP
.B show \fIjob_uuid\fR
Show a job and the state on each host: \fBok\fR, \fBfailed\fR, \fBrefused\fR or \fBpending\fR.
A \fBrefused\fR line is only shown when there is one (see \fBRefused jobs\fR).
.TP
.B results \fR[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIhost\fR] [\fIjob_uuid\fR]
Show the results of a job, or all results of a host.
The header line of each result names the state, in the same colours as \fBlist\fR: green when the job worked, yellow when the host refused it, red when it ran and did not work.
.TP
.B ack \fIjob_uuid\fR
Say that you have seen how a job went.
Without this a job that failed, was refused, or is still waiting for a host is never removed (see \fBHow long jobs are kept\fR).
.TP
.B cancel \fIjob_uuid\fR
Cancel a job. Hosts that did not run the job yet will not run it.
The job stays in the list and is shown as cancelled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-host \fIhost1,host2\fR
Hosts or nodes the job is for.
.TP
.B \-\-unit \fIunit\fR
All hosts of this unit.
.TP
.B \-\-recursive
With \fB\-\-unit\fR: also the hosts of all units below it.
.TP
.B \-\-role \fIrole1,role2\fR
All hosts of these roles.
A role holds hosts, never nodes, so this never reaches a node.
.TP
.B \-\-group \fIgroup1,group2\fR
All hosts of these groups.
A group holds hosts, never nodes.
.TP
.B \-\-arg \fIname=value\fR
An argument of the job. Use it once for each argument.
A value with commas is a list, for example \fBpackages=tcpdump,ngrep\fR.
The same name twice is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-file \fIpath\fR
The file that \fBfile_mgmt put_file\fR writes on the host.
.TP
.B \-\-key \fIpath\fR
The signing key of a repository for \fBrepo_mgmt add_repo\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-expiry \fItime\fR
How long hosts may run the job, for example \fB7D\fR.
The units are \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBD\fR, \fBW\fR, \fBM\fR and \fBY\fR, see
.BR otpme (7).
Default: 30 days.
A host that comes online later does not run an expired job.
.TP
.B \-\-on\-demand
Tell the hosts to look now.
Without it they find the job on their next round, which is at most a minute away.
.RS
.PP
A host told this way does not look at once.
A job for a unit is a job for every host in it, they are all told in the same second, and they would reach the node together \(em so each waits a moment of its own first, up to 10 seconds.
.PP
A \fBremote_access\fR job is the exception: it is for one host and somebody is waiting at a terminal for the shell, so that one is fetched at once.
.PP
It says how many hosts heard it.
A host that was not listening is not a failure \(em it asks on its next round \(em but it is why a job can take a minute to start.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-return\-uuid
Print the UUID of the job and nothing else.
For a script that has to go on working with the job; it is what
.BR otpme\-shell (1)
uses to find the host at the node.
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH SIGNATURES
A job signature covers the job type, the action, all arguments, the number of hosts and the name of each host.
These values are shown to you as tags before you sign.
A long value (more than 64 characters, for example file content) is shown as its size and its SHA256 hash.
You can compare the hash with \fBsha256sum\fR.
.PP
The host checks that the tags match the job.
So a node can not show you one job and send another job to the host.
.SH AUDIT LOG
A job is an order to run something on someone else's machine, so every step of it is written to the audit log.
Where that log goes is set with \fBAUDIT_LOG_SERVER\fR in \fIotpme.conf\fR.
Without a server the entries go to \fI/var/log/otpme/audit.log\fR on the machine that wrote them.
See
.BR otpme.conf (5).
.PP
On the nodes:
.TP
.B job_created
A job was made. Says the job type, the action, the arguments, the hosts, the signer and when the job expires.
The actor is the token that made it.
.TP
.B job_denied
Somebody asked for a job for a host they may not make jobs for.
Says the host and that \fBallow_confd_jobs\fR is the reason.
Written at warning level.
.TP
.B job_result
A host reported what became of a job.
Says the status and the exit code, and in \fBordered_by\fR the token the job was made with, so a result reads back to the order.
The actor is the host: it reports about itself, nobody is logged in there.
Warning level when the job did not work.
.TP
.B job_acked \fR/\fB job_cancelled
Somebody acknowledged a failed job, or took a job back.
.PP
On each host:
.TP
.B job_started
The host checked a job and is about to run it.
Says the signer it verified, by UUID.
.TP
.B job_done
The job finished on the host, with the status and the exit code.
Written once, when the result is there, even if sending it to the nodes has to be tried again.
Warning level when the job did not work.
.TP
.B job_refused
The host will not run a job, with the reason: no signer it trusts for this job type, a signature that does not hold, a runner that did not start, or an object the job names that has not reached the host yet.
Written at warning level.
The host writes this once per job, not once a round, so a job that waits for a sync does not fill the log.
.PP
Both ends name a host by its FQDN, so the entries of one job fit together.
.PP
A host names the signer by UUID and never by name.
It holds no users and no roles, only the UUIDs it trusts, and a signature carries no name either.
A name in such an entry would be one the host cannot check.
The name is on the nodes, in
.BR job_created ,
under the same job UUID.
.PP
Example of one job, from both sides:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mgmtd: Deploy job_created actor=alice/login job=8a1e1207\-... job_type=sw_mgmt \\
       action=upgrade args="packages=openssl" hosts=client1 host_count=1 \\
       signer=alice expiry=1790000000
confd: Deploy job_started actor=client1.example.org job=8a1e1207\-... \\
       job_type=sw_mgmt action=upgrade signer_uuid=fea7dc2d\-...
confd: Deploy job_done actor=client1.example.org job=8a1e1207\-... \\
       job_type=sw_mgmt action=upgrade status=ok exit_code=0
syncd: Deploy job_result actor=client1.example.org job=8a1e1207\-... \\
       job_type=sw_mgmt action=upgrade status=ok exit_code=0 \\
       ordered_by=alice/login
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
The examples use the realm \fBexample.org\fR and the site \fBberlin\fR.
An OID contains a \fB|\fR, so put the argument in quotes.
.SS Setup
Before the first job, two things are needed.
.TP
.B otpme-unit config hosts allow_confd_jobs 'role|example.org/berlin/host\-admins'
On a node: allow members of the role host\-admins to create jobs for the hosts of the unit \fBhosts\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type signer_mgmt 'role|example.org/berlin/host\-admins'
On each host, as root: trust the role host\-admins for signer_mgmt jobs.
After this, all other signers can be added with jobs (see \fBsigner_mgmt\fR below).
.SS Finding the hosts
A job needs to know which hosts it is for.
.BR otpme\-inventory (1)
answers that.
.PP
Which hosts still have the old openssl:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search 'openssl=3.0.9*'
client1 openssl=3.0.9\-1
client2 openssl=3.0.9\-1
.fi
.RE
.PP
The names of those hosts as one argument for
.BR \-\-host :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ otpme\-inventory search 'openssl=3.0.9*' | cut \-d' ' \-f1 | sort \-u | paste \-sd,
client1,client2
.fi
.RE
.PP
So the whole thing is two commands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hosts=$(otpme\-inventory search 'openssl=3.0.9*' | cut \-d' ' \-f1 | sort \-u | paste \-sd,)
$ otpme\-deploy add \-\-host "$hosts" \-\-arg packages=openssl sw_mgmt upgrade
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same works for hardware.
Upgrade the firmware package only on the machines that have that model:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hosts=$(otpme\-inventory search \-\-hardware 'model=PowerEdge R640' | cut \-d' ' \-f1 | paste \-sd,)
$ otpme\-deploy add \-\-host "$hosts" \-\-arg packages=srvadmin\-all sw_mgmt install
.fi
.RE
.PP
When the hosts are a unit, a role or a group, name that instead.
It stays right when a host is added later, and a list of names does not.
.SS sw_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts \-\-recursive \-\-arg packages=libreoffice sw_mgmt install
Install LibreOffice on all hosts of the unit \fBhosts\fR and the units below it.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg packages=firefox\-esr,thunderbird sw_mgmt upgrade
Upgrade two installed packages.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1,client2 \-\-arg packages=tcpdump,ngrep \-\-arg autoremove=yes sw_mgmt remove
Remove two packages and the packages that are no longer needed.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 sw_mgmt autoremove
Remove packages that are no longer needed.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts sw_mgmt dist_upgrade
Upgrade every package on all hosts of the unit \fBhosts\fR.
.SS repo_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg name=example \-\-arg 'line=deb https://repo.example.org/debian bookworm main' \-\-key example.asc repo_mgmt add_repo
Add an apt repository with its signing key from the file \fBexample.asc\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg name=example repo_mgmt del_repo
Remove the repository again.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts repo_mgmt update
Update the package lists.
.SS signer_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=sw_mgmt \-\-arg 'signer=role|example.org/berlin/sw\-admins' signer_mgmt add_signer
Trust the role sw\-admins for sw_mgmt jobs on client1.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts \-\-recursive \-\-arg type=service_mgmt \-\-arg 'signer=user|example.org/berlin/alice' signer_mgmt add_signer
Trust the user alice for service_mgmt jobs on all hosts of the unit \fBhosts\fR and the units below it.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=repo_mgmt \-\-arg 'signer=role|example.org/berlin/repo\-admins' \-\-arg tags=deploy,berlin signer_mgmt add_signer
Add a signer with two tags.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts \-\-recursive \-\-arg type=service_mgmt \-\-arg 'signer=user|example.org/berlin/alice' signer_mgmt update_signer
Pin the new key of alice, for example after alice changed the sign key.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=sw_mgmt \-\-arg 'signer=role|example.org/berlin/sw\-admins' signer_mgmt del_signer
Remove the signer again.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=sw_mgmt signer_mgmt show_signer
Ask client1 which sw_mgmt signers it holds.
The answer comes with \fBotpme\-deploy results\fR, and its first column is the UUID of each entry.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg type=sw_mgmt \-\-arg signer=fea7dc2d\-3c41\-4a0e\-9d2f\-1b6c8e5a7d90 signer_mgmt del_signer
Remove a signer by the UUID of its entry.
Use this where the OID does not say which entry you mean \(em a name that was given to a second object, or an entry \fBshow_signer\fR lists and you cannot place.
A signer whose user or role was deleted can be removed by its OID as well.
.SS service_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-unit hosts \-\-arg unit=cups.service service_mgmt restart
Restart CUPS on all hosts of the unit \fBhosts\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg unit=ssh.service service_mgmt enable
Start SSH at boot.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg unit=bluetooth.service service_mgmt stop
Stop a service.
.SS file_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg path=/etc/motd \-\-arg mode=0644 \-\-file motd.txt file_mgmt put_file
Write /etc/motd with the content of the local file \fBmotd.txt\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg path=/etc/app/app.conf \-\-arg mode=0640 \-\-arg owner=root \-\-arg group=app \-\-file app.conf file_mgmt put_file
Write a file with owner, group and mode. The directory must exist.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-arg path=/etc/motd file_mgmt del_file
Remove a file.
.SS host_mgmt
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-expiry 1h host_mgmt reboot
Reboot client1. If the host is offline for more than one hour, it does not reboot later.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy add \-\-host client1 \-\-expiry 10m host_mgmt shutdown
Switch client1 off. Give it a short expiry: a host that was offline and comes back should not switch itself off hours later.
.SS Jobs and results
.TP
.B otpme-deploy list
List all jobs.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy list \-\-host client1
List the jobs for client1.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy list \-\-pending
List the jobs that at least one host did not run yet.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy list \-\-failed
List the jobs that failed on at least one host.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy show 8a1e1207\-f626\-42ff\-b538\-60b7b4223f31
Show a job and its state on each host.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy results 8a1e1207\-f626\-42ff\-b538\-60b7b4223f31
Show the results of a job.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy results \-\-host client1
Show all results of client1.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy ack 8a1e1207\-f626\-42ff\-b538\-60b7b4223f31
Say that you have seen how a job went, so it can be removed.
.TP
.B otpme-deploy cancel 8a1e1207\-f626\-42ff\-b538\-60b7b4223f31
Cancel a job.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/otpme/confd/
Full output of each job, on the host.
.TP
.I /var/spool/otpme/confd/
Jobs, results and markers of confd, on the host.
